WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.077
MCP, the Model Context Protocol, is an open standard

00:00:04.077 --> 00:00:07.555
for plugging outside systems into an agent as tools.

00:00:07.905 --> 00:00:10.763
A browser, a database, your docs, a search engine.

00:00:11.113 --> 00:00:14.228
Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and many others support it.

00:00:15.628 --> 00:00:19.873
The agent talks to an MCP server, and the server talks to the system.

00:00:20.223 --> 00:00:23.722
Servers run on your machine, or remotely over HTTP.

00:00:24.072 --> 00:00:26.158
At the start, only tool names load.

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:31.581
Full definitions load when a tool is needed, so adding servers barely touches your context.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.622
Servers have scopes too:

00:00:33.622 --> 00:00:38.203
local first, then the project's .mcp.json, then your user config.

00:00:39.603 --> 00:00:42.761
These clips were fact-checked with a web search and scraping server.

00:00:43.111 --> 00:00:45.225
It searched and fetched the official docs,

00:00:45.225 --> 00:00:48.295
and the agent compared the wording before a line was written.

00:00:48.645 --> 00:00:51.155
Every claim traces back to an official page,

00:00:51.155 --> 00:00:53.551
and each clip lists its sources on screen.

00:00:53.901 --> 00:00:57.912
Without it, the agent works from what it remembers, which can be out of date.

00:00:59.312 --> 00:01:03.088
Each server is a new set of permissions, so add only what you trust.

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:07.086
Big tool results land in your context, and they count against it.

00:01:07.436 --> 00:01:08.858
Pair a server with a skill:

00:01:08.858 --> 00:01:11.703
the server gives access, the skill gives the know-how.

00:01:12.053 --> 00:01:16.074
Codex configures servers under mcp_servers in config.toml,

00:01:16.074 --> 00:01:18.431
and the same servers work in both.
